    "lawCode": "Add §390-l, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3563\n\nSPONSOR: SERRANO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto requiring the anchoring of furniture and electronics in child day\ncare centers, public institutions for children and certain other facili-\nties\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill protects children at childcare centers from the tipping of\nunsecured furniture.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n-Outlines the child care facilities covered under the legislation\n-Requires large furniture and electronics to be properly secured within\ncovered facilities\n-Authorizes the Office of Children and Family Services to take action if\na child care center doesn't immediately correct a violation of this act\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2013, Million Dollar Baby recalled 18,000 dressers and in 2016 Ikea\nrecalled 29 million dressers and chests due to the serious tip-over\nhazard that they possessed. Moreover, ASTM International, a standards\norganization that publishes voluntary consensus technical standards,\ndeveloped recommendations that furniture be sold with proper anchoring\nsystems to prevent tip over accidents. According to the United States\nConsumer Product Safety Commission, every 24 minutes a child goes to the\nemergency room with an injury resulting from a television or piece of\nfurniture tipping over. From 2000 to 2013, 84 percent of the 430 deaths\nfrom unanchored furniture involved children younger than the age of 10.\n\nDespite the clear danger that unsecured furniture can present to our\nchildren, and the relative ease in preventing these accidents, there are\nno current requirements for anchoring heavy dressers or tube style tele-\nvisions in child care centers throughout New York State.  This legis-\nlation would ensure that when we send our children to childcare, they\nare safe from this all too common hazard by requiring that child care\nfacilities properly secure or anchor furniture and televisions to the\nwall. Requiring daycare centers to make minimal financial investments\nusing commonly available products could save significant sums of money\nby preventing emergency room visits.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect 180 days after it shall have become a law.\n",
